-- Emails are case-insensitive ("Change email", Phase 6 pass 1 — decided with
-- the person 2026-10-22). Generated by drizzle-kit, then HAND-EDITED (marked
-- "HAND-EDITED" below), the same way as 0035.
--
-- Every email is now stored in ONE spelling: trimmed, A–Z lowercased
-- (common/email-address.ts, normalizeEmail — applied to every email a request
-- brings in). This CHECK is the database's half: whatever writes an email —
-- the app, the seed, a query typed by hand — can't store 'Jane@x.com'. With
-- one spelling per address, the existing UNIQUE (users_email_unique) means
-- "unique ignoring case".
--
-- COLLATE "C" pins lower() to A–Z, whatever the server's locale: the app's
-- rule exactly. (Postgres's lower() otherwise follows the database's locale —
-- on a C.UTF-8 or en_US.UTF-8 database it also lowercases 'É' — and the app
-- and the database must agree on which spelling is "normal".)
--
-- Why hand-edited: ADD CONSTRAINT … CHECK checks EVERY existing row and fails
-- the whole migration if one breaks it, and until now nothing lowercased an
-- email — a database may hold 'Jane@x.com'. Those rows are normalised first.
-- But normalising can make two rows EQUAL ('Jane@x.com' and 'jane@x.com'):
-- two accounts for one address, each with its own history. Which one keeps
-- the address is a human decision (merging accounts isn't something a
-- migration can guess), so the migration refuses to run and lists them.

-- HAND-EDITED (1/2): refuse to run while two accounts would end up with the
-- same address. RAISE EXCEPTION aborts the migration's transaction: nothing
-- below runs, nothing is changed, and drizzle-kit migrate prints the message.
-- The fix, by hand, for each pair: decide which account keeps the address,
-- give the other one a different email (`UPDATE users SET email = … WHERE id
-- = …`), then run the migration again.
--
-- The query groups the users by their normalised email — the CHECK's own
-- expression — and keeps the groups with more than one row; string_agg joins
-- each group's ids, then the groups, into one line of text.
DO $$
DECLARE
  collisions text;
BEGIN
  SELECT string_agg(format('%s (user ids %s)', normalized, ids), '; ')
  INTO collisions
  FROM (
    SELECT
      btrim(lower(email COLLATE "C")) AS normalized,
      string_agg(id::text, ', ' ORDER BY id) AS ids
    FROM users
    GROUP BY 1
    HAVING count(*) > 1
  ) AS same_address;

  IF collisions IS NOT NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'These accounts would share an email once emails ignore case: %', collisions
      USING HINT = 'For each, decide which account keeps the address, change the other''s email by hand, then run the migration again.';
  END IF;
END $$;--> statement-breakpoint

-- HAND-EDITED (2/2): every other email in the normalised spelling. The WHERE
-- is the CHECK's condition, negated: exactly the rows the CHECK would refuse
-- change, no more. The UNIQUE can't be hit here — the check above has just
-- proven no two rows normalise to the same address.
--
-- Runs as the table's owner (migrations do), so row security doesn't apply.
-- No audit entry: an audit row needs the USER who acted
-- (audit_logs.performed_by is NOT NULL), and a migration has none. This file,
-- in version control, is the record of the change, as for 0035's renamed
-- titles. The address itself doesn't change — only its capitals: the person
-- still receives their mail, and signs in with whatever capitals they type.
UPDATE "users"
SET "email" = btrim(lower("email" COLLATE "C"))
WHERE "email" <> btrim(lower("email" COLLATE "C"));--> statement-breakpoint

-- Generated: the CHECK itself.
ALTER TABLE "users" ADD CONSTRAINT "users_email_normalized" CHECK ("users"."email" = btrim(lower("users"."email" COLLATE "C")));